    "result_text": "The full test suite passes: **278 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors**. BUILD SUCCESS.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the city title-casing behavior in `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`:\n\n**`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 added city resolution during owner creation:\n- Added `owner.setCity(resolveCity(owner.getCity()))` before the customer code is assigned, so the resolved spelling flows into both the stored `city` field and the returned DTO.\n- `resolveCity(String city)` \u2014 looks for any existing owner whose city matches case-insensitively; if found, reuses that owner's **exact** stored spelling. Otherwise it title-cases the supplied value. Null-safe.\n- `titleCase(String value)` \u2014 upper-cases the first letter of each whitespace-separated word and lower-cases the rest, preserving original whitespace (e.g. `\"LONDON\"` \u2192 `\"London\"`, `\"new york\"` \u2192 `\"New York\"`).\n\n**`OwnerRestControllerV1Tests.java`** \u2014 added two tests:\n- `createOwnerTitleCasesCityWhenNoOwnerExistsInThatCity` \u2014 posts `\"new  YORK\"` (no existing owner) and asserts the returned city is `\"New  York\"`.\n- `createOwnerReusesExistingCitySpelling` \u2014 posts `\"mcfarland\"`, where seed data already has an owner in `\"McFarland\"`, and asserts the returned city reuses the exact spelling `\"McFarland\"` (which plain title-casing would incorrectly produce as `\"Mcfarland\"`).\n\nThe change composes cleanly with the existing customer-code logic (which already uppercases the city) \u2014 the `CARDIFF-0002` sequential-code test still passes.",
